 To retrieve the username from a JWT (JSON Web Token) in an ASP.NET C# WebApi project, follow these steps:

1. First, make sure you have installed the `Microsoft.IdentityModel.Tokens` NuGet package to work with JWT tokens in your project.

2. Create a method to validate and extract the username from the JWT token. Here's an example of how to create such a method:

```csharp
using System;
using System.IdentityModel.Tokens.Jwt;
using Microsoft.IdentityModel.Tokens;

public static string GetUsernameFromJwtToken(string jwtToken)
{
    try
    {
        // Parse the JWT token
        var handler = new JwtSecurityTokenHandler();
        SecurityToken securityToken;
        ClaimsPrincipal principal = handler.ValidateToken(jwtToken, "Your_Jwt_Secret_Key", out securityToken, ValidateAudience: false);

        if (principal != null && securityToken is JwtSecurityToken jwtTokenObject)
        {
            // Get username from first claim
            return jwtTokenObject.Claims.FirstOrDefault(x => x.Type == ClaimTypes.Name)?.Value;
        }
    }
    catch (Exception ex)
    {
        throw new ArgumentException("Invalid JWT token.", nameof(jwtToken));
    }

    return null;
}
```

Replace `"Your_Jwt_Secret_Key"` with your actual secret key used for signing and validating the JWT token.

3. Now, use this method whenever you need to extract the username from a JWT token passed as a request header or query string parameter. For example, when creating a controller action:

```csharp
[HttpGet]
public IActionResult GetUserData([FromQuery] string accessToken)
{
    string username = GetUsernameFromJwtToken(accessToken);

    if (username != null)
    {
        // Process the request based on the retrieved username
        // ...
    }
    else
    {
        return BadRequest("Invalid JWT token.");
    }
}
```

