 I'm here to help answer your question! In programming, the terms "inner functions" and "outer functions" refer to certain relationships between functions in functional programming and closures.

An outer function is the function that contains an inner function as a part of its body. The inner function has access to the variables and parameters of the outer function. This allows the inner function to use and manipulate those variables, even after the outer function has returned.

Here's an example of an outer function with an inner function:

```javascript
function outerFunction(x) {
  const innerFunction = function(y) {
    return x + y;
  };
  return innerFunction;
}
const addFive = outerFunction(5);
console.log(addFive(3)); // Output: 8
```

In this example, `outerFunction` is the outer function, and it returns the inner function `innerFunction`. When we call `outerFunction` with the argument `5`, it creates the inner function and assigns it to the variable `addFive`. The inner function then has access to the value of `x` (which is `5`), so when we call `addFive(3)`, it adds `3` to `5` and returns `8`.

