2. Use `useCombinedRefs` instead of using separate state variables for focusing input elements. This reduces unnecessary re-renders.

3. Use `useEffect` hook to invalidate the query when an update or deletion happens.

```jsx
useEffect(() => {
  if (upsertPrompt.isSuccess || deletePrompt.isSuccess) {
    utils.prompt.invalidate();
  }
}, [upsertPrompt.isSuccess, deletePrompt.isSuccess]);
```

4. Use `useMemo` to compute the sorted prompts array once and store it in a state variable.

```jsx
const sortedPrompts = useMemo(() => {
  return prompts.data && prompts.data.sort((a, b) => b.updatedAt.getTime() - a.updatedAt.getTime());
}, [prompts.data]);
```

With these optimizations, you can significantly improve the performance and efficiency of your code while maintaining its functionality.

